---
/**
 * "We filled these in from your last visit."
 *
 * Restoring a form silently is unsettling — a client who half-remembers
 * starting this cannot tell whether the answers on screen are theirs, ours, or
 * a mistake. So the page says what it did, when the answers are from, and
 * offers the one way out.
 *
 * Blue rather than neutral, which is the one place this page spends a colour on
 * something other than an error: on a portal page blue means "where things
 * currently stand", and a restored draft is exactly that. The treatment matches
 * the error summary so the two read as the same kind of object.
 *
 * role="status" rather than "alert": this is worth announcing when the page
 * settles, but it must not interrupt or take focus the way a failed submission
 * does.
 */
---

<div
	id="questionnaire-restored"
	role="status"
	hidden
	class="mb-10 border-l-2 border-state-current bg-state-current/[0.04] py-4 pr-5 pl-6"
>
	<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
		<p class="max-w-[58ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-charcoal/75" data-restore-message></p>

		{/* A real button, not a link: it changes state rather than going
		    anywhere, and it is the only destructive control on the page — so it
		    confirms before it clears. */}
		<button
			type="button"
			class="shrink-0 font-display text-[0.6875rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-label text-charcoal underline decoration-charcoal/30 underline-offset-4 transition-colors duration-200 hover:decoration-charcoal"
			data-start-fresh
		>
			Start fresh
		</button>
	</div>
</div>
